</figure>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Reading the table.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For each method, instantiate the GKPO object (Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 GKPO: A Canonical Interchange Schema ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>); if the <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reducibility</span> flags are empty,
collection yields <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> and margins match; otherwise GKPO records witnesses that certify non-reducibility.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Notes.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Divergence-modified objectives (e.g., f-DPO/VAR/EGPO) often introduce prompt-dependent reference terms or score-dependent weights;
GKPO faithfully represents these via <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">reference.form</span> and <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">weight.form</span> while preventing unsound conversions (Sec. <a href="#S5" title="5 Demonstrations (Worked Examples) ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>).
Dataset-level pipelines (RLAIF, Constitutional AI, HH) typically remain inside <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math> when their operators are additive on <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math>
or multiplicative on <math id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>w</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w</annotation></semantics></math>, in which case composition collapses under collection.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">7 </span>Why This Paper: Collapse, Extension, and De-cluttering</h2>

<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Collapse (normal form).</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Many RLHF objectives differ mainly in syntax. Opal provides a way to show that ladders built from
<em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">additive penalties</em> and <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiplicative weights</em> reduce to a single
normal form on pairwise margins whenever three assumptions hold: fixed reference,
additive penalties, and score-independent weights (Sec. <a href="#S2" title="2 Background and Operator Algebra ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, Thm. <a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Reduction). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>).
This <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collapse</em> helps clarify when objectives are effectively equivalent
(e.g., PPO-RM <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> DPO) and reduces ambiguity: if two proposals reduce to the same
<math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> (up to scale), they yield the same decisions and order-consistent losses.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Extension (a canonical schema).</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Not all proposals satisfy these assumptions. We introduce GKPO, a canonical schema
that (i) represents any pairwise objective; (ii) canonicalizes reducible cases to the normal form
with a deterministic <span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">opal_hash</span>; and (iii) records <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">explicit</em> flags and finite witnesses
when assumptions fail (reference shift, non-additive gate, score-dependent weight)
(Sec. <a href="#S4" title="4 GKPO: A Canonical Interchange Schema ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>). GKPO can serve as an interchange layer: method <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\to</annotation></semantics></math> GKPO <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\to" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\to</annotation></semantics></math> method <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>
preserves margins whenever the reduction preconditions hold.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">De-cluttering (taxonomy and witnesses).</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The operator view offers one way to organize the RLHF landscape (Sec. <a href="#S6" title="6 Method Taxonomy under Opal ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>): methods inside <math id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>
reduce to a normal form and differ only by interpretable operator choices; methods outside are
distinguished by <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">which</em> assumption they violate, illustrated by <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">finite witnesses</em>
(Sec. <a href="#S5" title="5 Demonstrations (Worked Examples) ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>). In practice, this can support clearer ablations and reproducibility:
we suggest reporting the GKPO JSON, the <span id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">opal_hash</span>, and any reducibility flags along with results.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Scope and non-goals.</h4>

<div id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Opal characterizes <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pairwise margin</em> objectives. It does not address optimizers, sampling,
or token-level credit assignment (e.g., MDP/token shaping); those are orthogonal. The focus here is to
standardize <em id="S7.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what is optimized</em> (operators, references, weights), not how training is carried out.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S8" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">8 </span>Discussion and Broader Impact</h2>

<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">What Opal explains.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Opal clarifies when seemingly distinct RLHF objectives are algebraically equivalent (same margins up to positive scaling)
and when they differ in decisions or losses (non-reducibility).
This perspective can help practitioners compare methods in terms of their <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">assumptions</em>, rather than naming or heuristics:
if a proposal satisfies fixed reference, additive penalties, and score-independent weights,
then its behavior is characterized by its normal form <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> (<a href="#Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1 (Reduction). ‣ 3 Theoretical Results ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>);
otherwise it can indicate which assumption does not hold and provide a finite witness.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">GKPO as an interchange schema.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The schema supports three practical goals:
(i) <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">comparability</em> (methods serialize to the same keys and units),
(ii) <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">convertibility</em> (reducible methods can be translated via GKPO without altering margins), and
(iii) <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">provenance</em> (the Opal hash makes operator choices auditable; <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Canonicalization and hashing ‣ 4 GKPO: A Canonical Interchange Schema ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>).
We view GKPO as a lightweight interchange layer for RLHF: it standardizes the <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interfaces</em> (scores, weights, references, penalties),
but not the training stack itself.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Practical guidance.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It can be useful to report, alongside results:
(1) the GKPO JSON (or a compact diff relative to DPO),
(2) reducibility flags/witnesses (if any), and
(3) the Opal hash.
For ablations, one option is to change <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">one operator</em> at a time (e.g., add a penalty or swap a weight factor) and
verify the hash changes—this helps reduce confounds and keeps comparisons interpretable.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Opal currently characterizes objectives defined on <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">pairwise margins</em>.
Listwise objectives, trajectory-level credit assignment, or
policies with stateful evaluators may require extending the operator alphabet
(e.g., sequence-level gates, context-coupled references).
Our analysis assumes strictly increasing links and losses evaluated on a scalar margin;
nonscalar couplings (e.g., multi-dimensional safety constraints <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chittepu et al., 2025</a>)</cite>) remain outside scope.
Finally, GKPO represents <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what</em> is optimized (operators), not <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> it is optimized
(optimizers, sampling, reward models).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Threats to validity.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Outside <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, two operator stacks can agree on a dataset yet diverge under distribution shift;
witnesses demonstrate <em id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">existence</em> of disagreement, not its frequency.
Even inside <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, poor scale choices (temperature <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math>) can affect optimization despite
invariance of decisions; canonical scale fixing (<a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Canonicalization and hashing ‣ 4 GKPO: A Canonical Interchange Schema ‣ Opal: An Operator Algebra View of RLHF" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>) can mitigate this, but
reporting the actual <math id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> used remains informative.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Broader impact.</h4>

<div id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S8.SS0.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Standardizing operator reporting and convertibility may support reproducibility,
reduce redundant proposals, and highlight genuine innovations.
At the same time, easier recombination of operators could make it simpler to assemble mis-specified objectives;
we therefore emphasize explicit declaration of reducibility flags and finite witnesses,
and encourage publishing GKPO artifacts alongside results.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S9" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">9 </span>Related Work</h2>

<div id="S9.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S9.p1.1" class="ltx_p">PPO-RLHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Christiano et al., 2017</a>)</cite> established KL-regularized policy optimization with a reward model. DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rafailov et al., 2023</a>)</cite> rewrote the objective as a direct preference loss with a fixed reference. Variants include ORPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hong et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, <math id="S9.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>χ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi</annotation></semantics></math>PO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Han et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, VAR <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Winata et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, EGPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhou et al., 2025</a>)</cite>, RRHF <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Yuan et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, KTO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ethayarajh et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, and GRPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. We study operator structure: when ladders collapse and explicit limits where they do not (Appendix F surveys more).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">10 </span>Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S10.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We introduced Opal, an operator algebra for RLHF that expresses objectives as ladders of
<em id="S10.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">additive penalties</em> and <em id="S10.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">multiplicative weights</em> on a base score, and described a
reduction law that holds under three assumptions (fixed reference, additive penalties, score-independent weights),
yielding a normal form on pairwise margins. This algebra highlights when proposals collapse to the same
<math id="S10.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>w</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f^{\ast},w^{\ast})</annotation></semantics></math> (up to positive scaling) and when they differ through finite witnesses
(reference shift, non-additive gates, score-dependent weights). Building on this, we proposed
GKPO, an interchange schema that canonicalizes reducible methods, records explicit
flags and witnesses outside <math id="S10.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℛ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{R}</annotation></semantics></math>, and attaches a deterministic <span id="S10.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">opal_hash</span> for
provenance. Our demonstrations illustrate conversions among common objectives
(e.g., PPO-RM <math id="S10.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> DPO under assumptions) and stress tests (SHIFT/GATE/SCORE) that surface
non-reducibility. A lightweight reference implementation accompanies the paper.</p>
</div>
<section id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Practical takeaway.</h4>

<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It can be helpful to report the GKPO JSON (or a compact “delta” w.r.t. DPO), the <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">opal_hash</span>, and any reducibility
flags/witnesses alongside empirical results. When claiming equivalence, stating the three preconditions
(fixed reference, additive penalties, score-independent weights) or providing a witness otherwise
makes ablations clearer and comparisons more transparent.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Limitations.</h4>

<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis is margin-based and pairwise; listwise preferences, trajectory-level credit assignment,
and stateful evaluators would require extending the operator alphabet. We assume strictly increasing links and
scalar losses; vector-valued constraints (e.g., multi-criteria safety) remain outside scope. Finally, GKPO
captures <em id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">what</em> is optimized (operators), not <em id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">how</em> it is optimized (samplers, optimizers).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Future work.</h4>

<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">(i) Extend Opal/GKPO beyond pairwise margins to listwise/sequence settings and token-level shaping;
(ii) formalize dataset-stage composition with richer operators (e.g., learned group weights, dynamic
references, multi-annotator heterogeneity); (iii) develop statistical tests that infer reducibility
or produce witnesses from logs; (iv) integrate GKPO artifacts into common stacks (e.g., <span id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">trl</span>)
and public leaderboards; (v) explore categorical or convex-analytic formulations that generalize the
reduction law to broader objective classes.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Outlook.</h4>

<div id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S10.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">By separating algebra (operators and assumptions) from engineering (optimizers and pipelines),
Opal and GKPO aim to encourage standardized reporting, enable sound conversions, and support clearer comparisons.
We view GKPO artifacts as a lightweight companion to RLHF papers—small, auditable, and useful for checking
equivalence claims and reproducing operator choices.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
